<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19869824</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2131942</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>ABSORPTION FROM THE PERICARDIAL CAVITY</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Drinker</surname><given-names>Cecil K.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Field</surname><given-names>Madeleine E.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x3281440N0x38b77d8">From the Department of Physiology, Harvard School of Public Health, Boston</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>1</month><year>1931</year></pub-date><volume>53</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>143</fpage><lpage>150</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>19</day><month>9</month><year>1930</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1931, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> 1. Physiological salt solution is absorbed from the pericardial cavity of medium-sized rabbits at a rate of approximately 1.3 cc. per hour. This absorption is via the subepicardial blood capillaries. 2. Rabbit serum and horse serum are absorbed extremely slowly&#x02014;an indication of the low-grade lymphatic drainage of the pericardial sac. 3. Graphite particles of bacterial dimensions are also removed very slowly. Such particles enter lymphatics only after phagocytosis. The lymphatics in the basal part of the pericardium are the principal source of drainage. Subepicardial lymphatics are entered with difficulty from the pericardial cavity. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>